sh4, ze0: copy the kernel debug strings to serial port 0 (TODO M8.B6).

EU2011 nk.exe never writes its debug text to a UART: every NKDbgPrintfW
ends in fn 0x880404f4 with the formatted UTF-16 string in r4, which goes
to an AstLog buffer (0x88044478) and, only when the words at 0x89e613b0
and 0x89e613b4 are both 1, to a "DebugLog" log handle (0x8804048c ->
0x88044fe8).  Neither path touches the debug SCIF at 0xffe46000, whose only
writer (0x8804cfa4) is the AER exception reporter (M8.A6, C-1281/C-1282).
So no gate value or boot argument can put the text on the serial port.

target/sh4: a translate-time hook.  When sh4_dbgmsg_va is non-zero, the
instruction at that VA first calls helper_dbgmsg, which reads the
NUL-terminated UTF-16 string at r4 (bank 1 when SR.MD and SR.RB) through
cpu_memory_rw_debug (no guest exception, the gdbstub's path) and passes it
as UTF-8 (max 1024 units) to sh4_dbgmsg_sink.  System emulation only; no
guest byte is changed.

hw/sh4/ze0.c: machine option dbgmsg=VA (uint32, default 0 = off) sets the
hook and a sink that writes to serial_hd(0), the chardev of the debug SCIF;
a string that does not end in '\n' gets one, so each message is a line.
EU2011: dbgmsg=0x880404f4 (tools/qemu-ze0/run.sh --dbgmsg, its default).

Evidence and controls: docs/experiments/EXP-2026-11-20-M8.B6-debuglog-uart.md
(C-6240..C-6243).  Base: QEMU 8.2.10 + the earlier patches (sorts after
ze0-0006).

